<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> was a short-lived state proclaimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> in October 1918 by the South Slav lands leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. Governed by a national council, it united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, Croatia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. On 1 December 1918 it joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> to form the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs.jpg" alt="State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Orlovic: I drew this flag in photoshop, on the base of another version on the internet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_State_of_Slovenes,_Croats_and_Serbs.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The South Slavic peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> were divided among several administrative units. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-littoral">Austrian Littoral</a>, the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a> and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> lay under Austrian jurisdiction, while the Kingdom of Croatia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Fiume</a> were under Hungarian jurisdiction, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> was a condominium. On 30 May 1917 the Yugoslav Club, a group of Croatian and Slovene deputies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsrat">Reichsrat</a>, adopted the May Declaration, which sought the unification of all lands of the monarchy inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> in one independent entity.</p><p>A meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> on 2 and 3 March 1918 produced the Zagreb Resolution, which proclaimed the unity of the three peoples and demanded self-determination. In the summer, people's organisations were formed in Split, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susak">Sušak</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Ljubljana</a>. The People's Council was established in Zagreb on 5 to 8 October 1918 with 95 representatives, and on 19 October it declared itself the supreme representative body of all South-Slavic peoples in the monarchy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The state was proclaimed on 29 October 1918. Its first president was the Slovene Anton Korošec, with the Serb Svetozar Pribićević and the Croat Ante Pavelić as vice presidents. It aspired to include all the Austro-Hungarian territories inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, but Serbs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a> formed their own administration under the Serbian National Board in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novi-sad">Novi Sad</a>, and Vojvodina joined the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> on 25 November 1918. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Syrmia</a> had seceded and joined Serbia a day earlier.</p><p>Emperor Karl assigned the Austro-Hungarian Navy and its harbours to the National Council, but the Italian navy attacked and dismembered the fleet. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-villa-giusti">Armistice of Villa Giusti</a>, signed on 4 November 1918, allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to occupy large parts of the state's territory, and Italian troops held Istria and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> until the Treaty of Rapallo took effect in 1921. The state was never recognised internationally.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In a note of 31 October the National Council told the governments of the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> that it intended to form a common state with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>. On 23 and 24 November it declared unification, but its instructions were largely ignored by the delegation, which negotiated with Regent Alexander instead. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stjepan-radic">Stjepan Radić</a>'s Peasant Party called the move foolish and argued that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-parliament">Croatian Parliament</a> had never approved it.</p><p>On 1 December 1918 Regent Alexander proclaimed the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, thirty-three days after the state's proclamation. The National Council had ceased to operate and never formally ratified the proclamation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In late October 1918 the empire dissolved from within: Czechoslovakia declared independence, the <b>State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</b> was proclaimed, and Hungary ended the union, while the Italian victory at Vittorio Veneto led to the armistice of Villa Giusti on 3 November.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 1 December 1918 Serbia united with the newly created <b>State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</b> to form the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes, later known as the Kingdom of Yugoslavia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>As Austria-Hungary collapsed in October 1918, a National Council in Zagreb proclaimed the <b>State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</b>, which, threatened by Italian advances along the Adriatic, sought rapid union with Serbia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
